{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "```python\ndef is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if an integer is an undulating number based on its digit pattern.\n    An undulating number must have at least three digits, with its first two digits different, and subsequent digits alternating.\"\"\"\n\n    digits = str(number)\n\n    # Return False if the number has fewer than 3 digits.\n    if len(digits) < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Return False if the first two digits are identical.\n    if digits[0] == digits[1]:\n        return False\n\n    # Iterate from the third digit to verify the alternating pattern.\n    for i in range(2, len(digits)):\n        if digits[i] != digits[i-2]:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "```python\ndef is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if an integer is an undulating number, meaning its digits strictly alternate\n    between two distinct values (e.g., 1212121).\n    \"\"\"\n    num_str = str(number)\n\n    if len(num_str) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    first_digit = num_str[0]\n    second_digit = num_str[1]\n\n    if first_digit == second_digit:\n        return False\n\n    for i in range(2, len(num_str)):\n        if i % 2 == 0:  # Even index, should match the first digit\n            if num_str[i] != first_digit:\n                return False\n        else:  # Odd index, should match the second digit\n            if num_str[i] != second_digit:\n                return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
