{"task_id": "Mbpp/96", "solution": "```python\ndef find_number_of_divisors(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of positive divisors for a given integer n.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer for which to find the number of divisors.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The count of positive divisors.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If n is not an integer.\n        ValueError: If n is 0 (number of divisors is undefined).\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Type check for n (BR1).\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be an integer.\")\n\n    # 2. Handle n == 0 (BR5, UC1 A2).\n    if n == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Number of divisors is undefined for 0.\")\n\n    # 3. Handle negative n by taking absolute value (UC1 A3, documented choice).\n    #    This ensures we count positive divisors for the magnitude of the number.\n    abs_n = abs(n)\n\n    # 4. Iterate from 1 up to sqrt(abs(n)) to find divisors (BR2, BR3, BR4).\n    #    Initialize count to 0.\n    count = 0\n    i = 1\n    while i * i <= abs_n:\n        if abs_n % i == 0:\n            # If i is a divisor, then abs_n / i is also a divisor.\n            # If i*i == abs_n, then i and abs_n/i are the same (perfect square),\n            # so we count it only once.\n            if i * i == abs_n:\n                count += 1\n            else:\n                count += 2 # Count both i and abs_n/i\n        i += 1\n\n    # 5. Return the calculated count.\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/98", "solution": "```python\ndef multiply_num(numbers: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of all numbers in a list and divides by the list's length.\n    Returns the result as a float.\n    \"\"\"\n    product = 1.0\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ZeroDivisionError(\"Cannot divide by zero. The input list is empty.\")\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if not isinstance(num, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the list must be numbers.\")\n        product *= num\n\n    length = len(numbers)\n    result = product / length\n    return result\n```"}
