{"task_id": "Mbpp/123", "solution": "```python\n# Module for calculating amicable numbers sum.\n\ndef sum_proper_divisors(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of proper divisors for a given positive integer n.\n    Proper divisors are positive divisors of n, excluding n itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n\n    total_sum = 1  # 1 is always a proper divisor for n > 1\n    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            total_sum += i\n            if i * i != n:\n                total_sum += n // i\n    return total_sum\n\ndef amicable_numbers_sum(upperLimit: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of all amicable numbers up to and including the specified upper limit.\n    An amicable pair (a, b) consists of two distinct positive integers such that\n    the sum of the proper divisors of 'a' equals 'b' and the sum of the proper divisors of 'b' equals 'a'.\n\n    Args:\n        upperLimit: An integer representing the inclusive upper bound for finding amicable numbers.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the sum of all amicable numbers found up to upperLimit.\n    \"\"\"\n    if upperLimit < 1:\n        return 0\n\n    amicable_set = set()\n    for a in range(1, upperLimit + 1):\n        if a in amicable_set:\n            continue\n\n        sum_div_a = sum_proper_divisors(a)\n\n        # Optimization: b must be greater than a and within limit to be a valid candidate.\n        # Also, check if sum_div_a is not 'a' itself to avoid perfect numbers pairing with themselves.\n        if sum_div_a > a and sum_div_a <= upperLimit:\n            sum_div_b = sum_proper_divisors(sum_div_a)\n\n            if sum_div_b == a:\n                # Found an amicable pair (a, sum_div_a)\n                amicable_set.add(a)\n                amicable_set.add(sum_div_a)\n\n    return sum(amicable_set)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/123", "solution": "def _sum_proper_divisors(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Helper function to calculate the sum of proper divisors for a given integer n.\n    Proper divisors include 1 but exclude n itself.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n    s = 1\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of n to find divisors efficiently.\n    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            s += i\n            # If i*i != n, then n // i is a distinct divisor.\n            if i * i != n:\n                s += n // i\n    return s\n\ndef _is_amicable_pair(a: int, b: int, sum_div_a: int, sum_div_b: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Helper function to determine if two numbers form an amicable pair.\n    It checks if sum_div_a equals b, and sum_div_b equals a, ensuring they are distinct.\"\"\"\n    return sum_div_a == b and sum_div_b == a and a != b\n\ndef amicable_numbers_sum(limit: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of all amicable numbers less than or equal to a specified limit.\n    It iterates through numbers, identifies amicable pairs, and sums them up.\"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    # Keep track of numbers already added to the sum to prevent double-counting.\n    found_amicable_numbers = set()\n\n    for n in range(1, limit + 1):\n        # Skip if this number has already been processed as part of an amicable pair.\n        if n in found_amicable_numbers:\n            continue\n\n        sum_div_n = _sum_proper_divisors(n)\n\n        # Check conditions for a potential amicable pair:\n        # sum_div_n must be different from n and within the limit.\n        if sum_div_n > 0 and sum_div_n != n and sum_div_n <= limit:\n            sum_div_sum_div_n = _sum_proper_divisors(sum_div_n)\n\n            # Use the helper to confirm if (n, sum_div_n) form an amicable pair.\n            if _is_amicable_pair(n, sum_div_n, sum_div_n, sum_div_sum_div_n):\n                # If they are an amicable pair, add both numbers to the total sum.\n                total_sum += n\n                total_sum += sum_div_n\n                # Mark both numbers as found to avoid reprocessing or double-counting.\n                found_amicable_numbers.add(n)\n                found_amicable_numbers.add(sum_div_n)\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/124", "solution": "```python\nimport cmath\nfrom typing import Union\n\n\ndef angle_complex(real_component: Union[int, float], imaginary_complex_component: complex) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the angle (phase) of a complex number formed from real and imaginary components.\n    It combines them and uses cmath.phase() to compute the angle in radians.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Combine the real and imaginary components into a single complex number.\n    combined_complex_number = real_component + imaginary_complex_component\n\n    # Compute the angle (phase) of the combined complex number using cmath.phase().\n    angle_in_radians = cmath.phase(combined_complex_number)\n\n    return angle_in_radians\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/109", "solution": "```python\ndef _rotate_binary_string(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Rotates the input string s once by moving the first character to the end.\n    This helper performs a single left rotation.\n    \"\"\"\n    return s[1:] + s[0]\n\ndef _binary_to_decimal(binary_str: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Converts a binary string binary_str to its base-10 integer value.\n    This uses Python's built-in integer conversion with base 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    return int(binary_str, 2)\n\ndef _is_odd(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Determines if the integer n is odd.\n    It returns True if n is odd, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return n % 2 != 0\n\ndef odd_Equivalent(binary_string: str, rotations: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the number of odd decimal equivalents resulting from a specified number of rotations of a binary string.\n    It orchestrates string rotation, binary-to-decimal conversion, and odd number checking.\n    \"\"\"\n    odd_count = 0\n    current_binary_string = binary_string\n\n    for _ in range(rotations):\n        decimal_equivalent = _binary_to_decimal(current_binary_string)\n        if _is_odd(decimal_equivalent):\n            odd_count += 1\n        current_binary_string = _rotate_binary_string(current_binary_string)\n\n    return odd_count\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/125", "solution": "```python\ndef find_length(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum difference between '0's and '1's in any substring of a binary string.\n    This uses Kadane's algorithm after transforming '0's to 1 and '1's to -1.\n    \"\"\"\n    transformed_array = []\n    for char in s:\n        if char == '0':\n            transformed_array.append(1)\n        else:  # char == '1'\n            transformed_array.append(-1)\n\n    if not transformed_array:\n        return 0\n\n    max_so_far = transformed_array[0]\n    current_max = transformed_array[0]\n\n    for x in transformed_array[1:]:\n        current_max = max(x, current_max + x)\n        max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n\n    return max_so_far\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/126", "solution": "```python\ndef sum_common_divisors(num1: int, num2: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of common divisors for two positive integers.\n    It iterates up to the minimum of the two numbers to find common divisors.\n    \"\"\"\n    common_divisors = set()\n    limit = min(num1, num2)\n\n    for i in range(1, limit + 1):\n        if num1 % i == 0 and num2 % i == 0:\n            common_divisors.add(i)\n\n    total_sum = sum(common_divisors)\n\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/125", "solution": "```python\ndef find_length(binary_string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum difference between the number of 0s and 1s in any substring.\n    It transforms the binary string into an array of integers (0->1, 1->-1) and applies Kadane's algorithm.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not binary_string:\n        return 0\n\n    values = []\n    for char in binary_string:\n        if char == '0':\n            values.append(1)\n        else: # char == '1'\n            values.append(-1)\n\n    max_so_far = values[0]\n    current_max = values[0]\n\n    for i in range(1, len(values)):\n        current_max = max(values[i], current_max + values[i])\n        max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n\n    return max_so_far\n```"}
