{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "```python\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of possible sequences of length n given constraints.\n    Each element must be positive, greater than or equal to twice the previous element, and less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    memo = {}\n\n    def _count_sequences_recursive(current_m: int, current_n: int, current_length: int, last_element: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Recursively counts valid sequences for the remaining length, starting from `last_element`.\n        Uses memoization to store results of subproblems to avoid redundant calculations.\n        \"\"\"\n        if (current_length, last_element) in memo:\n            return memo[(current_length, last_element)]\n\n        if current_length == current_n:\n            return 1\n\n        total_count = 0\n        start_next_element = 2 * last_element\n\n        for next_element in range(start_next_element, current_m + 1):\n            total_count += _count_sequences_recursive(current_m, current_n, current_length + 1, next_element)\n\n        memo[(current_length, last_element)] = total_count\n        return total_count\n\n    total_overall_sequences = 0\n    # Iterate through all possible starting elements for the sequence.\n    for first_element in range(1, m + 1):\n        if n == 1:\n            total_overall_sequences += 1\n        else:\n            total_overall_sequences += _count_sequences_recursive(m, n, 1, first_element)\n\n    return total_overall_sequences\n```"}
