{"task_id": "Mbpp/255", "solution": "```python\nimport itertools\n\ndef combinations_colors(input_list: list, n: int) -> list[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all possible combinations with repetition of elements from input_list,\n    where each combination has exactly n elements.\n\n    Args:\n        input_list: A list of elements of any type.\n        n: An integer representing the desired length of each combination.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of tuples, with each tuple representing a unique combination.\n    \"\"\"\n    # itertools.product generates cartesian product, which is equivalent to combinations with repetition\n    # when the order matters (i.e., (A,B) is different from (B,A)).\n    # The problem implies order matters as it asks for \"combinations (with repetition)\".\n    # To handle potential duplicates in the input_list and ensure unique tuples in the output\n    # while adhering to \"combinations (with repetition)\", we use the input_list directly.\n    # The use of list(set()) or similar for unique_elements_ordered is not explicitly stated in the prompt,\n    # and \"combinations (with repetition) of the elements of the list\" typically implies using\n    # the elements as provided. If uniqueness of *elements chosen* was implied, itertools.combinations_with_replacement\n    # on unique elements would be used, but the design suggests product.\n\n    # According to the design's \"Collect all unique tuples into a list\",\n    # we should generate all combinations and then ensure uniqueness of the resulting tuples.\n    # itertools.product inherently handles repetition of elements and produces tuples.\n    # We will then convert the list of tuples to a set and back to a list to ensure uniqueness\n    # of the resulting combination tuples if duplicates arise from product due to identical\n    # source elements.\n\n    all_combinations_with_repetition = list(itertools.product(input_list, repeat=n))\n\n    # Convert to a set to remove duplicate tuples, then back to a list.\n    # This aligns with the design's \"Collect unique combinations\" and \"Return list of unique combination tuples\".\n    unique_combinations = list(set(all_combinations_with_repetition))\n\n    # The order of combinations might not be preserved after converting to a set.\n    # If the order of combinations in the output list needs to be deterministic and based on\n    # the order generated by itertools.product, a different approach (e.g., manually checking for uniqueness\n    # during generation or after) would be needed. However, the problem statement and example\n    # do not specify order requirements for the final list of tuples, only that each combination is a tuple.\n\n    return unique_combinations\n```"}
