{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\ndef max_Product(array_of_integers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies the pair of integers with the maximum product from a given array.\n    Returns an empty tuple if the array has fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(array_of_integers) < 2:\n        return ()\n\n    # Sort the array to efficiently find the two largest and two smallest numbers.\n    sorted_arr = sorted(array_of_integers)\n\n    # The two largest numbers are at the end of the sorted array.\n    max1, max2 = sorted_arr[-1], sorted_arr[-2]\n\n    # The two smallest numbers are at the beginning of the sorted array.\n    min1, min2 = sorted_arr[0], sorted_arr[1]\n\n    # Calculate the products of the two candidate pairs.\n    product_of_two_largest = max1 * max2\n    product_of_two_smallest = min1 * min2\n\n    # Compare the products and return the pair that yields the maximum.\n    if product_of_two_largest >= product_of_two_smallest:\n        return (max2, max1)\n    else:\n        return (min1, min2)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\ndef max_Product(numbers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"Finds the pair with the highest product from a given list of integers.\n    Raises ValueError if the input list contains fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list must contain at least two elements to form a pair.\")\n\n    max_product = float('-inf')\n    max_pair = (0, 0) # Initialize with a dummy pair, will be updated.\n\n    for i in range(len(numbers)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(numbers)):\n            num1 = numbers[i]\n            num2 = numbers[j]\n            current_product = num1 * num2\n\n            if current_product > max_product:\n                max_product = current_product\n                max_pair = (num1, num2)\n\n    return max_pair\n```"}
