{"task_id": "Mbpp/463", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef max_subarray_product(nums: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum product of a contiguous subarray.\n    This function handles positive, negative, and zero integers in O(N) time.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize max_so_far, max_ending_here, and min_ending_here with the first element.\n    # The problem constraints imply nums will not be empty for base initialization.\n    max_so_far = nums[0]\n    max_ending_here = nums[0]\n    min_ending_here = nums[0]\n\n    for i in range(1, len(nums)):\n        num = nums[i]\n\n        # Store the current max_ending_here before it's updated, as it's needed for min_ending_here calculation.\n        temp_max_ending_here = max_ending_here\n\n        # Update max_ending_here: it can be the current number, or current number times existing max/min product.\n        max_ending_here = max(num, temp_max_ending_here * num, min_ending_here * num)\n        # Update min_ending_here: it can be the current number, or current number times existing max/min product.\n        min_ending_here = min(num, temp_max_ending_here * num, min_ending_here * num)\n\n        # Update the overall maximum product found so far.\n        max_so_far = max(max_so_far, max_ending_here)\n\n    return max_so_far\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\ndef max_product(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence within a list of integers.\n    Utilizes dynamic programming to track maximum products ending at each element.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize dp array where dp[i] stores the max product of an increasing subsequence ending at nums[i].\n    # Initially, each element itself forms an increasing subsequence of length 1.\n    dp = list(nums)\n    # Initialize max_overall_product with the largest single element,\n    # to account for cases where the max product is just one number (or all are negative).\n    max_overall_product = max(nums)\n\n    n = len(nums)\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If nums[i] can extend an increasing subsequence ending at nums[j]\n            if nums[i] > nums[j]:\n                # Update dp[i] to be the maximum of its current value\n                # or the product of dp[j] and nums[i].\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * nums[i])\n\n        # After considering all preceding elements for nums[i], update the overall maximum product.\n        max_overall_product = max(max_overall_product, dp[i])\n\n    return max_overall_product\n```"}
