{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "```cpp\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm> // For std::min\n\n\n/**\n * @brief Finds the k-th smallest element recursively from two sorted arrays.\n * This private helper function implements the core binary search logic.\n */\ntemplate <typename T>\nT find_kth_recursive_helper(const std::vector<T>& arr1, int low1, int high1,\n                            const std::vector<T>& arr2, int low2, int high2,\n                            int k) {\n    // Ensure arr1 (conceptually) is the array with fewer remaining elements\n    // to simplify calculations and reduce recursive depth in worst cases.\n    if ((high1 - low1 + 1) > (high2 - low2 + 1)) {\n        return find_kth_recursive_helper(arr2, low2, high2, arr1, low1, high1, k);\n    }\n\n    // Base case: If the first array is exhausted, the k-th element must be in the second array.\n    if (low1 > high1) {\n        return arr2[low2 + k - 1];\n    }\n\n    // Base case: If k is 1, return the minimum of the first elements from the current ranges.\n    if (k == 1) {\n        return std::min(arr1[low1], arr2[low2]);\n    }\n\n    // Calculate how many elements to take from each array's current range.\n    // 'i' is the number of elements from arr1, capped by k/2 and arr1's remaining size.\n    int i = std::min(k / 2, high1 - low1 + 1);\n    // 'j' is the remaining number of elements needed to sum to k.\n    int j = k - i;\n\n    // Compare the elements at the chosen partition points.\n    if (arr1[low1 + i - 1] < arr2[low2 + j - 1]) {\n        // If arr1's element is smaller, it means the k-th element cannot be\n        // among the first 'i' elements of arr1's current range. Discard them.\n        return find_kth_recursive_helper(arr1, low1 + i, high1, arr2, low2, high2, k - i);\n    } else if (arr1[low1 + i - 1] > arr2[low2 + j - 1]) {\n        // If arr2's element is smaller, discard the first 'j' elements of arr2's current range.\n        return find_kth_recursive_helper(arr1, low1, high1, arr2, low2 + j, high2, k - j);\n    } else {\n        // If elements are equal, this element is the k-th smallest.\n        return arr1[low1 + i - 1];\n    }\n}\n\n\n/**\n * @brief Finds the k-th smallest element from two given sorted arrays.\n * It assumes valid sorted inputs and a valid 1-indexed k.\n */\ntemplate <typename T>\nT find_kth(const std::vector<T>& arr1, const std::vector<T>& arr2, int k) {\n    // As per critical requirements, no exception handling for k is performed here.\n    // The design specifies k-th as 1-indexed.\n    return find_kth_recursive_helper(arr1, 0, arr1.size() - 1, arr2, 0, arr2.size() - 1, k);\n}\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "```python\ndef _sieve_of_eratosthenes(limit: int) -> set[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds all prime numbers up to a given limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes.\n    Returns a set containing these prime numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if limit < 2:\n        return set()\n\n    is_prime = [True] * (limit + 1)\n    is_prime[0] = is_prime[1] = False\n\n    for p in range(2, int(limit**0.5) + 1):\n        if is_prime[p]:\n            for multiple in range(p * p, limit + 1, p):\n                is_prime[multiple] = False\n\n    primes = {i for i, status in enumerate(is_prime) if status}\n    return primes\n\n\ndef get_ludic(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates and returns a list of \"lucid numbers\" (1 and prime numbers) up to n.\n    The list includes 1 if n >= 1, and all primes p such that 1 < p <= n, in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 1:\n        return []\n\n    ludic_list = [1]\n\n    # Get all primes up to n efficiently.\n    primes_set = _sieve_of_eratosthenes(n)\n\n    # Add primes to the list in ascending order, starting from 2.\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        if i in primes_set:\n            ludic_list.append(i)\n\n    return ludic_list\n```"}
