{"task_id": "Mbpp/759", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\nclass DecimalValidator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates if a string represents a decimal number with exactly two decimal places.\n    This class encapsulates the core validation logic for the system.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Regex to match a string that is an optional sign, followed by digits and a decimal point,\n    # or just a decimal point, and then exactly two digits after the decimal point.\n    _DECIMAL_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"^-?(\\d*\\.\\d{2}|\\d+\\.\\d{2})$\")\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate(input_string: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if the given string is a decimal number with exactly two digits of precision.\n        It uses a regular expression to perform robust pattern matching.\n        \"\"\"\n        return bool(DecimalValidator._DECIMAL_PATTERN.fullmatch(input_string))\n\ndef is_decimal(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides the public API to validate if a string is a decimal number with two decimal places.\n    This function delegates the actual validation logic to the DecimalValidator class.\n    \"\"\"\n    return DecimalValidator.validate(s)\n```"}
