{"task_id": "Mbpp/767", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\ndef get_pairs_count(numbers: List[Union[int, float]], target_sum: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of pairs in a list whose elements sum up to a target value.\n    Handles duplicate numbers correctly, counting each valid pair combination.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    num_counts = Counter(numbers)\n\n    # Tracks numbers already accounted for to prevent double counting.\n    # Each number is processed once as the starting point of a pair.\n    processed_nums = set()\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if num in processed_nums:\n            continue\n\n        complement = target_sum - num\n\n        # If the complement is the same as the current number, we need at least two occurrences.\n        if complement == num:\n            # Calculate combinations: n * (n - 1) / 2 for identical numbers.\n            if num_counts[num] >= 2:\n                count += (num_counts[num] * (num_counts[num] - 1)) // 2\n            processed_nums.add(num) # Mark as processed after accounting for all combinations.\n        # If the complement is different, count pairs by multiplying their frequencies.\n        elif complement in num_counts and num_counts[complement] > 0:\n            count += num_counts[num] * num_counts[complement]\n            processed_nums.add(num) # Mark both numbers as processed.\n            processed_nums.add(complement)\n\n    return count\n```"}
